      "latin": "Tibi habē.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "Keep it (for yourself).",
      "evidence": [
        "Mart. 10.51.16: <span lang=\"la\">quae tua sunt, tibi habē</span>",
        "Mart. 13.53.2: <span lang=\"la\">et cocleās tibi habē</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "<span lang=\"la\">Sibi habēre</span>, to keep a thing for oneself (Lewis &amp; Short s.v. <span lang=\"la\">habeō</span>). In the poets it often waves a thing away (“keep your snails”); said with the change held out, it is a kindness. The change is <span lang=\"la\">nummulī</span> or <span lang=\"la\">quod reliquum est</span>."
    }
  ],
  "phrases": [
    {
      "latin": "Quod reliquum est, tibi habē.",
      "english": "Keep the change.",
      "source": "composed"
    },
    {
      "latin": "Hōs nummulōs tibi habē.",
      "english": "Keep these coins; they’re for you.",
      "source": "composed"
    },
    {
      "latin": "quae tua sunt, tibi habē: quae mea, redde mihi",
      "english": "keep what’s yours; give me back what’s mine",
      "source": "Mart. 10.51.16"
    }
  ],
